{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Problem\nNothing validates, before a reconcile pass mutates a live client Jira, that the mapped\nprojects actually EXIST / are visible to the bridge bot. Two live-only failure modes:\n1. A mapped-but-nonexistent (or decommissioned / permission-lost) project key is\n   syntactically valid, so `_validate_project_key` passes; the failure only surfaces\n   deep in fan-out as an ACLI error. This is exactly the `05b8-4efe-8d58-4c27` incident class.\n2. `resolve_project` (projects_store.py) returns `mapping.legacy_default` VERBATIM,\n   unvalidated. Every unbound/legacy ticket (absent bridge_project) resolves there. The\n   cross-project write guard's allowed set = `read_projects().keys()` and EXCLUDES creates\n   (applier.py `_cross_project_targets`), so a legacy_default pointing at a real-but-wrong\n   (or nonexistent) project drives a mass CREATE storm into the wrong place, unguarded.\n\n## Approach\nAdd a reconcile-pass PREFLIGHT that runs BEFORE any outbound mutation and validates that\nevery project the pass could touch is visible to the configured backend.\n\nRequired-key set (single source of truth, mirrors the fetcher fan-out at fetcher.py:540 so\nthe preflight validates exactly what fan-out queries): `read_projects(repo_root).keys()`;\nwhen that mapping is EMPTY the fetcher falls back to the single configured backend\n`query_project` (`[_query_project]`) — the exact green-state incident shape\n`{legacy_default:'REB', projects:{}}` — so the preflight INCLUDES that same fallback\n`query_project` when `projects` is empty. PLUS `legacy_default` (when non-empty), since\n`resolve_project` sends every unbound/legacy create there. Empty-and-no-fallback → nothing\nto check (a no-op that never probes).\n\nProbe transport: the check reuses `access_check.py`'s existing `AcliClient` transport (the\nsame Jira Cloud transport `run_access_check` already drives) and its retry helper\n(`_retry_sleep` / `JQL_RETRY_COUNT`). It enumerates bot-visible project keys through the\nclient's ALREADY-PRESENT generic REST accessor `AcliClient._direct_rest_get`\n(acli_rest.py:207 — the same helper used by `get_issue_property`, `transition_issue`,\n`validate_assignee_exists`) against the standard Jira Cloud \"Get projects paginated\" endpoint\n`GET /rest/api/3/project/search`, walking pages via `startAt` and the response envelope's\n`values[].key` / `isLast`. Pagination terminates safely on `isLast`, on an empty `values`\npage, or at a hard page cap (defence against a server that never sets `isLast`); no new\ntransport class or subprocess is introduced.\n\nRetry vs hard fail: a transient/retryable transport error is retried up to `JQL_RETRY_COUNT`\nwith the `_retry_sleep` backoff; if the probe still cannot reach the backend it raises a\ndistinct `TransportUnavailableError` (fail-closed, its own message), kept separate from\n`ProjectVisibilityError` (a reachable backend that simply does not list a required key).\n\nReusable single source of truth: the helper is an importable function in `access_check.py`\n(`check_mapped_project_visibility(repo_root, *, client=..., query_project=..., ...)` returning\na structured `ProjectVisibilityResult` with `status` in {`ok`,`missing`,`transport_unavailable`,\n`skipped`}, plus an `enforce_*` wrapper that raises). Ticket `9702` (bridge fsck live\ndiagnostic) reuses this exact function — it is NOT inlined into the reconcile pass.\n\nBackend-neutrality: `access_check.py` is Cloud/ACLI-specific and this probe is a Cloud probe.\nThe WIRING GATES ON THE CONFIGURED BACKEND: it reads\n`rebar.config.load_config().reconciler.backend` and only builds the Cloud `AcliClient` and\nruns the probe when that key is `\"jira\"`. For any other backend (e.g. `\"jira-datacenter\"`,\nwhose pycontribs transport exposes no `_direct_rest_get`) it logs an observable\n\"skipped: backend <key> has no visible-projects probe\" and returns without building a Cloud\nclient. As defence in depth the helper also returns `status=\"skipped\"` if handed a client\nlacking a callable `_direct_rest_get`.\n\nWiring point + result classification: wired at `__main__.py` `run_pass_result`, STRICTLY\nBEFORE `reconcile.reconcile_once` (the only outbound-mutation call), and only for\nmutating passes — read-only passes are skipped by concrete pre-`reconcile_once` signals:\n`route == \"preview\"` (preview), and `target_mode.value in {\"dry-run\",\"reconcile-check\"}`\n(`reconcile-check` is already short-circuited earlier in `main()`; `no_write` is computed\nINSIDE `reconcile_once`, after this point, so it is NOT used here). A preflight failure is\nCONVERTED INTO A CLASSIFIED `PassResult` (`_Disposition.OPERATIONAL_FAILURE`, exit 1) exactly\nlike the existing `reconcile_once` path — it is never an uncaught raise, so the\n`run_pass` / `_lib_ops.py` consumers that expect a returned `PassResult` do not crash. On a\nmissing/invisible project the message names the offending key(s) and the fix (correct\nprojects.json / legacy_default); it is observable via stderr/log.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A pass with a mapped key not visible to the bot aborts at preflight with a clear\n      message naming the offending key BEFORE any Jira write — `reconcile.reconcile_once` is\n      NOT called and `run_pass_result` returns an `OPERATIONAL_FAILURE` `PassResult`. Verified\n      by `python -m pytest tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_preflight_project_visibility.py -q`.\n- [ ] `legacy_default` (when non-empty) is included in the visibility check and an invisible\n      `legacy_default` is caught, naming it. Verified by the same test module.\n- [ ] When `projects` is empty, the configured `query_project` fallback is included in the\n      check, so an invisible fallback is caught (the green-state incident shape). Verified by\n      the same test module.\n- [ ] An all-visible mapping passes preflight and the pass proceeds unchanged\n      (`reconcile.reconcile_once` is called; no happy-path behavior change). Verified by the\n      same test module.\n- [ ] A hard auth/transport error fails closed with a distinct transport-unavailable message\n      (not project-not-visible) and is observable via a log/stderr message. Verified by the\n      same test module.\n- [ ] A non-Cloud configured backend (`config.reconciler.backend != \"jira\"`) and a client\n      lacking `_direct_rest_get` are both skipped (observable log) and the pass proceeds\n      without building a Cloud client. Verified by the same test module.\n\n## Testing\n- `python -m pytest tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_preflight_project_visibility.py -q` —\n  fake/injected transport (no live Jira). Helper-level: a fake client whose `_direct_rest_get`\n  returns `project/search` pages listing `{GOOD}` visible / `BAD` absent → `enforce_*` raises\n  `ProjectVisibilityError` naming `BAD`; an all-visible mapping → `status=\"ok\"`;\n  `legacy_default=WRONG` (not visible) is caught; empty `projects` + `query_project=FALL` not\n  visible is caught; a `_direct_rest_get` that raises → `TransportUnavailableError`\n  (fail-closed); a client without `_direct_rest_get` → `status=\"skipped\"`; multi-page\n  `values` are all collected. Wiring-level (`run_pass_result` with a stubbed\n  `reconcile_once`): missing key → `reconcile_once` NOT called + `OPERATIONAL_FAILURE`;\n  transport-unavailable → NOT called + `OPERATIONAL_FAILURE`; all-visible → `reconcile_once`\n  called once; `route==\"preview\"`, `target_mode.value==\"dry-run\"`, and a non-`\"jira\"` backend\n  each skip the probe and let the pass proceed. RED first: without the preflight the pass\n  proceeds to fan-out (`reconcile_once` is called on an invisible key).\n- Live boundary: no unit test hits a live Jira (transport is injected/faked); the real\n  `GET /rest/api/3/project/search` envelope/pagination is verified in unit tests against the\n  documented Jira Cloud response shape, and live verification is deferred to the existing\n  `rebar bridge access-check` / staging reconcile path (same transport, same credentials).\n- `make lint` and `make typecheck` from the worktree.\n\n## Sequencing\nLives in `access_check.py` (new importable, reusable mapped-projects + legacy_default +\nfallback visibility helper consuming the existing `_direct_rest_get` accessor) + wiring at\nthe pass entrypoint (`__main__.py` `run_pass_result`). Disjoint from A/B/C/E files. Reads\nprojects_store (no edit)."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786814819877727001,"uuid":"3a3bf81d-3d11-455b-96b3-f7601d80baf6"}